{"title":"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution(Quick,_fast,_easy!_Under_7_min_and_30_seconds!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution(Quick, fast, easy! Under 7 min and 30 seconds!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Each vertex of a regular octagon is independently colored either red or blue with equal probability. The probability that the octagon can then be rotated so that all of the blue vertices end up at positions where there had been red vertices is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/5/c85ea1ad780a799bdaf8f327e664f65eb6f3546b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac{m}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"18\" height=\"20\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. Find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that the question's condition mandates all blues to go to reds, but reds do not necessarily have to go to blue. Let us do casework on how many blues there are. \n</p><p>If there are no blues whatsoever, there is only one case. This case is valid, as all of the (zero) blues have gone to reds. (One could also view it as: the location of all the blues now were not previously red.) Thus, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>If there is a single blue somewhere, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cases - where can the blue be? Each of these is valid. \n</p><p>If there are two blues, again, every case is valid, and there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/4/724c9cb7e661e5b7ff6135362222c3b06fc64628.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dbinom82=28$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"81\" height=\"55\" /> cases. \n</p><p>If there are three blues, every case is again valid; there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/2/952647d14d3a6e56bf471b0f03a5c97a8e575abe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dbinom83=56$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"81\" height=\"55\" /> such cases. \n</p><p>The case with four blues is trickier. Let us look at all possible subcases. \n</p><p>If all four are adjacent (as in the diagram below), it is obvious: we can simply reverse the diagram (rotate it by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> units) to achieve the problem's condition. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possible ways to have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> adjacent blues, so this subcase contributes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/2/742da5665944424c5123c30b68655aa4708d3608.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import graph; unitsize(1cm); filldraw(circle((0.5,1.207),2.5),green,black); void oct11(int[] pts) { pair[] vertices = {(0,0),(1,0),(1.707,0.707),(1.707,1.707),(1,2.414),(0,2.414),(-0.707,1.707),(-0.707,0.707)}; draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1.707,0.707)--(1.707,1.707)--(1,2.414)--(0,2.414)--(-0.707,1.707)--(-0.707,0.707)--cycle); for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i+=1) { if (pts[i] == 0) { dot(vertices[i], blue+5); } if (pts[i] == 1) { dot(vertices[i], red+5); } } }; int[] sus = {0,0,0,0,1,1,1,1}; oct11(sus); [/asy]\" width=\"238\" height=\"238\" />\n</p><p>If three are adjacent and one is one away (as shown in the diagram below), we can not rotate the diagram to satisfy the question. This subcase does not work. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/a/b9ab557e30ba482d702a842b47bb3fdd314afc86.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import graph; unitsize(1cm); filldraw(circle((0.5,1.207),2.5),orange,black); void oct11(int[] pts) { pair[] vertices = {(0,0),(1,0),(1.707,0.707),(1.707,1.707),(1,2.414),(0,2.414),(-0.707,1.707),(-0.707,0.707)}; draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1.707,0.707)--(1.707,1.707)--(1,2.414)--(0,2.414)--(-0.707,1.707)--(-0.707,0.707)--cycle); for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i+=1) { if (pts[i] == 0) { dot(vertices[i], blue+5); } if (pts[i] == 1) { dot(vertices[i], red+5); } } }; int[] sus = {0,0,0,1,0,1,1,1}; oct11(sus); [/asy]\" width=\"238\" height=\"238\" />\n</p><p>If three are adjacent and one is two away, obviously it is not possible as there is nowhere for the three adjacent blues to go. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/1/92115d7c6e1e1e7c3f23d260afdaeba8c70ef76b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import graph; unitsize(1cm); filldraw(circle((0.5,1.207),2.5),orange,black); void oct11(int[] pts) { pair[] vertices = {(0,0),(1,0),(1.707,0.707),(1.707,1.707),(1,2.414),(0,2.414),(-0.707,1.707),(-0.707,0.707)}; draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1.707,0.707)--(1.707,1.707)--(1,2.414)--(0,2.414)--(-0.707,1.707)--(-0.707,0.707)--cycle); for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i+=1) { if (pts[i] == 0) { dot(vertices[i], blue+5); } if (pts[i] == 1) { dot(vertices[i], red+5); } } }; int[] sus = {0,0,0,1,1,0,1,1}; oct11(sus); [/asy]\" width=\"238\" height=\"238\" />\n</p><p>If there are two adjacent pairs that are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> apart, it is not possible since we do not have anywhere to put the two pairs. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/2/ca217140eca3a7b81a5d0f2becc23d8f4758fcfc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import graph; unitsize(1cm); filldraw(circle((0.5,1.207),2.5),orange,black); void oct11(int[] pts) { pair[] vertices = {(0,0),(1,0),(1.707,0.707),(1.707,1.707),(1,2.414),(0,2.414),(-0.707,1.707),(-0.707,0.707)}; draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1.707,0.707)--(1.707,1.707)--(1,2.414)--(0,2.414)--(-0.707,1.707)--(-0.707,0.707)--cycle); for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i+=1) { if (pts[i] == 0) { dot(vertices[i], blue+5); } if (pts[i] == 1) { dot(vertices[i], red+5); } } }; int[] sus = {0,0,1,0,0,1,1,1}; oct11(sus); [/asy]\" width=\"238\" height=\"238\" />\n</p><p>If there are two adjacent pairs that are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> apart, all of these cases are possible as we can rotate the diagram by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> vertices to work. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of these cases. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/2/d22ec2fb746e740a5e40b218d3ea764912749bf7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import graph; unitsize(1cm); filldraw(circle((0.5,1.207),2.5),green,black); void oct11(int[] pts) { pair[] vertices = {(0,0),(1,0),(1.707,0.707),(1.707,1.707),(1,2.414),(0,2.414),(-0.707,1.707),(-0.707,0.707)}; draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1.707,0.707)--(1.707,1.707)--(1,2.414)--(0,2.414)--(-0.707,1.707)--(-0.707,0.707)--cycle); for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i+=1) { if (pts[i] == 0) { dot(vertices[i], blue+5); } if (pts[i] == 1) { dot(vertices[i], red+5); } } }; int[] sus = {0,0,1,1,0,0,1,1}; oct11(sus); [/asy]\" width=\"238\" height=\"238\" />\n</p><p>If there is one adjacent pair and there are two separate ones each a distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> from the other, this case does not work. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/e/13e855976c0f740c1af225e50f60e1e56b0303eb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import graph; unitsize(1cm); filldraw(circle((0.5,1.207),2.5),orange,black); void oct11(int[] pts) { pair[] vertices = {(0,0),(1,0),(1.707,0.707),(1.707,1.707),(1,2.414),(0,2.414),(-0.707,1.707),(-0.707,0.707)}; draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1.707,0.707)--(1.707,1.707)--(1,2.414)--(0,2.414)--(-0.707,1.707)--(-0.707,0.707)--cycle); for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i+=1) { if (pts[i] == 0) { dot(vertices[i], blue+5); } if (pts[i] == 1) { dot(vertices[i], red+5); } } }; int[] sus = {0,0,1,0,1,0,1,1}; oct11(sus); [/asy]\" width=\"238\" height=\"238\" />\n</p><p>If we have one adjacent pair and two separate ones that are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> away from each other, we can flip the diagram by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> vertices. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of these cases. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/e/acea992c1667f75da1167fdc9bf2488d747bd4f2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import graph; unitsize(1cm); filldraw(circle((0.5,1.207),2.5),green,black); void oct11(int[] pts) { pair[] vertices = {(0,0),(1,0),(1.707,0.707),(1.707,1.707),(1,2.414),(0,2.414),(-0.707,1.707),(-0.707,0.707)}; draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1.707,0.707)--(1.707,1.707)--(1,2.414)--(0,2.414)--(-0.707,1.707)--(-0.707,0.707)--cycle); for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i+=1) { if (pts[i] == 0) { dot(vertices[i], blue+5); } if (pts[i] == 1) { dot(vertices[i], red+5); } } }; int[] sus = {0,0,1,0,1,1,0,1}; oct11(sus); [/asy]\" width=\"238\" height=\"238\" />\n</p><p>Finally, if the red and blues alternate, we can simply shift the diagram by a single vertex to satisfy the question. Thus, all of these cases work, and we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> subcases. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/a/1dade03e0f200716a043a23d75db0e7461b19fc1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import graph; unitsize(1cm); filldraw(circle((0.5,1.207),2.5),green,black); void oct11(int[] pts) { pair[] vertices = {(0,0),(1,0),(1.707,0.707),(1.707,1.707),(1,2.414),(0,2.414),(-0.707,1.707),(-0.707,0.707)}; draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1.707,0.707)--(1.707,1.707)--(1,2.414)--(0,2.414)--(-0.707,1.707)--(-0.707,0.707)--cycle); for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i+=1) { if (pts[i] == 0) { dot(vertices[i], blue+5); } if (pts[i] == 1) { dot(vertices[i], red+5); } } }; int[] sus = {0,1,0,1,0,1,0,1}; oct11(sus); [/asy]\" width=\"238\" height=\"238\" />\n</p><p>There can not be more than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues, so we are done. \n</p><p>Our total is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/7/a7769deb5f24ceee3e673a1a77c73168eb62c419.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+8+28+56+8+4+8+2=115$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"300\" height=\"15\" />. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/e/a5efb3bc415def343ddc4f461d5d608c5dd6c548.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^8=256$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"70\" height=\"17\" /> possible colorings, so we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/e/49e1571f1deac076743747c1e7b8793bb20a1b1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{115}{256}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"33\" height=\"38\" /> and our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/8/3d825c14a915db2dac86fcfb2a634c3ec2536dd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$115+256=\\boxed{371}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"141\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~Technodoggo\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> be the number of red vertices and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> be the number of blue vertices, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/e/a2e97d5c174840f669f08f9d107ffbc434d1c146.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r+b=8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"16\" />. By the Pigeonhole Principle, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a3ac8f9ee56b41327f2157f6bdd51d6cc6cc07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r\\geq{b} \\Longrightarrow b\\leq4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"123\" height=\"17\" /> if a configuration is valid.\n</p><p>We claim that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/b/58b20e6a8bd0b272384f499a443590499d6e5383.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b\\leq3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"17\" />, then any configuration is valid. We attempt to prove by the following:\n</p><p>If there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/8/bb8ff0c7671a163262baab99fdf374e89e33c0b6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[b\\in{0,1,2}\\]\" width=\"72\" height=\"16\" /> vertices, then intuitively any configuration is valid. For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/6/626beed3a7eee094ce4b6cf79a49d81669f72d99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b=3$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" />, we do cases:\n</p><p>If all the vertices in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> are non-adjacent, then simply rotating once in any direction suffices. If there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> adjacent vertices, then WLOG let us create a set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/2/bd2cc78486e6f0af38339acf2efb1aa868848021.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{b_1,b_2,r_1\\cdots\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"110\" height=\"20\" /> where the third <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/f/daf4b756601df3422c1b49b0012b42d82119cc0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" /> is somewhere later in the set. If we assign the set as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/0/d80821d0b6e5a96c845501a211d5a4c82d16aee8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/4/7846ed7058056f9aaae058a2e1844970b531dff6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_3\\leq4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" />, then intuitively, rotating it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> will suffice. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/9/aa97a4526eb126a28859190ba1285a7bec532e22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_3=5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" />, then rotating it by 2 will suffice. Consider any other <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/9/f79324e5ff05bd54c02eab6140eb8b8be23a325f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_3&gt;5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" /> as simply a mirror to a configuration of the cases.\n</p><p>Therefore, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/b/58b20e6a8bd0b272384f499a443590499d6e5383.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b\\leq3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"17\" />, then there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/6/95653e4360ff53e1e0a3eb403272303643a4274c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{3}{\\binom{8}{i}}=93$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"110\" height=\"55\" /> ways. We do count the [i]degenerate[/i] case.\n</p><p>Now if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/a/fda5328bc4703ff88e62aa83ea58ae4e8361f5ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" />, we do casework on the number of adjacent vertices.\n0 adjacent: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/d/00d660c6970d1939ba49787c5b1b21fc82012b83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{b_1,r_1,b_2,r_2\\cdots{r_4}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"149\" height=\"20\" />. There are 4 axes of symmetry so there are only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/8/d68480d1dcba92047ee3b48e90e130c5fb7c8852.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{8}{4}=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"48\" height=\"38\" /> rotations of this configuration.\n</p><p>1 adjacent: WLOG <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/6/c86c682787d758476978c61cb0b5aa7476c15499.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{b_1,b_2\\cdots{b_3}\\cdots{b_4}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"144\" height=\"20\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/e/14e81f2f04979adf13a0a8423571af25e7baf6c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_4\\neq{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />. Listing out the cases and trying, we get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/c/25c37edbbc7d952ed7dae4bf85a38048031bb739.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_3=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/2/902714bbfc51ace0fbd0c66527b776323870f042.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_4=7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" /> is the only configuration. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways to choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/f/69fc4b9ed6d75d026557e7b06c2ed483c6ed0eda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/b/93b7aa611365e135f58aa09d52043ecd34855fe4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" /> and the rest is set, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways.\n</p><p>2 adjacent: We can have WLOG <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/d/6ed9f5102be9d250b43e67501e22f15c8a39e0e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{b_1,b_2\\cdots{b_3},b_4\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"124\" height=\"20\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/8/3d84ea7a1614dda768ad303a8cff478dc32f8692.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{b_1,b_2,b_3\\cdots\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"109\" height=\"20\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/e/14e81f2f04979adf13a0a8423571af25e7baf6c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_4\\neq{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />. The former yields the case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/9/aa97a4526eb126a28859190ba1285a7bec532e22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_3=5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/5/005cb43ffa2ec15c05056e3bbbf136e566db9b70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_4=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" /> by simply rotating it 2 times. The latter yields none. There are 2 axes of symmetry so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/3/013c042bde87793484f97b693700de23fc967cb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{8}{2}=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"46\" height=\"37\" /> configurations.\n</p><p>3 adjacent: WLOG <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/4/044ff4ef3e7549f0315ee5d2cc602b9300572034.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{b_1,b_2,b_3,b_4\\cdots\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"133\" height=\"20\" /> which intuitively works. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> configurations here as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/f/69fc4b9ed6d75d026557e7b06c2ed483c6ed0eda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" /> can is unique.\n</p><p>In total, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/a/fda5328bc4703ff88e62aa83ea58ae4e8361f5ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/4/0c46a409e78d2096c85dca220138e3729a6699d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2+8+4+8=22$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"147\" height=\"15\" /> configurations.\n</p><p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/2/d4258439c620ed873bb47874ede8aeb21d4f5b3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$22+93=115$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"112\" height=\"15\" /> configurations in total. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/e/a5efb3bc415def343ddc4f461d5d608c5dd6c548.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^8=256$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"70\" height=\"17\" /> total cases, so the probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/c/7ec6ba5be7a9bedb8247d2ce272308d295609f22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{115}{256}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"33\" height=\"38\" />. Adding them up, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/8/3d825c14a915db2dac86fcfb2a634c3ec2536dd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$115+256=\\boxed{371}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"141\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution.28Quick.2C_fast.2C_easy.21_Under_7_min_and_30_seconds.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution(Quick,_fast,_easy!_Under_7_min_and_30_seconds!)\">Video Solution(Quick, fast, easy! Under 7 min and 30 seconds!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution(Quick, fast, easy! Under 7 min and 30 seconds!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/gSCWnHfxxvY\">https://youtu.be/gSCWnHfxxvY</a>\n~MC\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/QnOatg7af7s\">https://youtu.be/QnOatg7af7s</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2\">Video Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nf80-0Eo72E&amp;t=4s&amp;ab_channel=MegaMathChannel\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nf80-0Eo72E&amp;t=4s&amp;ab_channel=MegaMathChannel</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I\" title=\"2024 AIME I\">2024 AIME I</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Answer_Key\" title=\"2024 AIME I Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2024\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 10\">Problem 10</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2024 AIME I Problems/Problem 15\">15</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AIME_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AIME Problems and Solutions\"> All AIME Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018024615\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.050 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.077 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 477/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1790/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 112/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 17694/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   20.197      1 -total\n 51.88%   10.478      1 Template:AIME_box\n 46.61%    9.414      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:26509:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018024615 and revision id 248221. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}